---
/**
 * /youtube-api/quota (keyword map C97, W1). Google facts: developers.google.com/youtube/v3/getting-started and the
 * revision history, checked 2026-11-12. Siar facts: docs/approvals/google.md; youtube.ts lines 60–68.
 * Never "100 uploads a day" (strike-plan.md §2 row 7): Google's allocation is quoted as Google writes it.
 */
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import SiarBox from '../../components/network/SiarBox.astro';
import StatusLine from '../../components/network/StatusLine.astro';
import NetFacts from '../../components/network/NetFacts.astro';
import { CHECKED, LIVE } from '../../components/network/data';

const GS = 'https://developers.google.com/youtube/v3/getting-started';
const REV = 'https://developers.google.com/youtube/v3/revision_history';
const hpw = (a: string) => (LIVE.howPublishing ? `/how-publishing-works#${a}` : '/docs/errors');
const faq = [
  { q: 'What is the YouTube Data API daily quota?', a: 'Google gives each project a default allocation of 10,000 units a day for most endpoints, with separate allocations for <code>videos.insert</code> and <code>search.list</code> calls. Every request costs at least one unit, including an invalid one. The allocation belongs to the Google Cloud project, not to a channel or a user.' },
  { q: 'What does YouTube API error quotaExceeded mean?', a: "It means the Google Cloud project behind the app has spent its daily quota. The request returns 403 with reason <code>quotaExceeded</code>. Wait for the daily reset, cut the calls that spend units without need (polling, repeated list calls), or ask Google for more quota with the quota extension form." },
  { q: 'How do I get more YouTube API quota?', a: "Fill in Google's quota extension request form for YouTube API Services. Google reviews how the project uses the API against its developer policies as part of the request. Siar submitted its request on 15 October 2026; Google acknowledged it the next day, and it is still in review." },
  { q: 'Is the YouTube Data API free?', a: "Google's getting-started page publishes a quota, not a price: the project gets its default allocation each day, and more needs an approved extension. Siar does not charge per YouTube upload; uploads count as posts in Siar's plan quota like any other network." },
];
---
<Article
  metaTitle="YouTube Data API quota and quotaExceeded, explained"
  h1="YouTube Data API quota, explained"
  description="How YouTube Data API quota works in 2026: the 10,000-unit default, the separate upload allocation, quotaExceeded, extensions, and how Siar handles it."
  kicker="YouTube API"
  updated={CHECKED}
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Platforms', href: '/platforms' }, { name: 'YouTube API', href: '/youtube-api' }, { name: 'Quota', href: '/youtube-api/quota' }]}
  lead={'Each Google Cloud project gets a daily YouTube Data API quota: 10,000 units for most endpoints, plus separate allocations for uploads (<code>videos.insert</code>) and searches. When it runs out, calls fail with <code>quotaExceeded</code> until the reset. Siar, a <a href="/">unified social media API</a>, uploads through its own project, so its customers share that quota.'}
  faq={faq}
  actions="quickstart"
>
  <StatusLine type="YOUTUBE" slug="youtube" />

  <h2 id="default">The default quota</h2>
  <p>Google's getting-started page sets the allocation per project, per day:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Allocation</th><th>What it covers</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>10,000 units</td><td>All endpoints except the two below, combined</td></tr>
      <tr><td>100 <code>videos.insert</code> calls</td><td>Uploads, in their own bucket</td></tr>
      <tr><td>100 <code>search.list</code> calls</td><td>Searches, in their own bucket</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <NetFacts facts={[
    { text: 'Google states a default allocation of "100 <code>search.list</code> calls, 100 <code>videos.insert</code> calls, and 10,000 units per day combined for all other endpoints", and "All API requests, including invalid requests, incur at least a one-point quota cost."', url: GS, source: 'Google, YouTube Data API getting started' },
  ]} />
  <p>Two things follow. First, a call is not an upload that viewers can see: a failed or rejected insert still spends from the bucket. Second, the allocation belongs to the project. An app that uploads for many channels through one project shares one allocation across all of them.</p>

  <h2 id="quota-exceeded">403 quotaExceeded: what to do</h2>
  <p>When the project has spent its allocation, YouTube answers 403 with the reason <code>quotaExceeded</code>. Retrying sooner fails the same way.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Wait for the reset.</strong> Google's getting-started page does not state the reset time; Siar treats it as midnight Pacific time.</li>
    <li><strong>Spend fewer units.</strong> Poll processing status less often, cache channel and playlist lists, and never call <code>search.list</code> to find your own uploads.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask for more.</strong> Use the quota extension form for the project. See <a href="/youtube-api/audit">YouTube API audit and the 2 November 2026 change</a> for what the review covers.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The <a href="/youtube-api/errors#code-403-quotaexceeded">YouTube API error codes</a> page has this entry with <code>uploadLimitExceeded</code>, the per-channel limit that looks similar but belongs to the channel, not the project.</p>

  <h2 id="siar">YouTube quota in Siar</h2>
  <p>Every Siar customer's YouTube calls go through Siar's Google Cloud project, so they share its quota. Siar asked Google for more on 15 October 2026, with its API audit: 100,000 units a day for the shared bucket and a larger upload bucket. Google acknowledged the request on 16 October 2026; nothing has come back since.</p>
  <SiarBox items={[
    'On <code>quotaExceeded</code>, Siar waits until the quota resets at midnight Pacific time and tries again on its own, up to three attempts in all.',
    'The post shows "The YouTube API quota for this project is used up for today. It resets at midnight Pacific time."',
    `Other networks in the same post still publish: <a href="${hpw('errors')}">the network's own error</a>.`,
  ]} />

  <h2 id="changes">What changed in 2026</h2>
  <p>Google's revision history dates two changes that make older quota articles out of date: uploads got their own <code>videos.insert</code> bucket on 1 June 2026, and on 2 November 2026 uploads from projects that have not passed the API audit stopped being forced to private.</p>
  <NetFacts facts={[{ text: 'The revision history lists the separate <code>videos.insert</code> quota and, on 2 November 2026, that uploads from unverified API projects "are no longer restricted to private viewing mode".', url: REV, source: 'Google, YouTube Data API revision history' }]} />
  <p class="small">Related: <a href="/youtube-api">YouTube upload API guide</a> · <a href="/youtube-api/audit">YouTube API audit</a> · <a href="/youtube-api/errors">YouTube API error codes</a> · <a href="/platforms">Network status</a>{LIVE.approvals && <> · <a href="/platforms/approvals#youtube">YouTube approval record</a></>}</p>
</Article>
